From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: use trylock instead of blocking lock in the
 interactive-boost hot path (#5)

Applies ON TOP of adios-android14-6.1-v3.2.0.patch. Same base as the
previous revision (v3.3.0-SAGA, parts 1-6 + all prior fixes -- see
commit history). Single, targeted efficiency change found by
re-reviewing the hottest code paths specifically (every sync read
insert), not by adding new mechanism.

CHANGE: task_was_idle_and_gets_boost() (used from add_to_dl_tree() for
every synchronous READ insert -- the single most frequent call in the
whole scheduler on a read-heavy workload) now uses
spin_trylock_irqsave() instead of a blocking spin_lock_irqsave() to
access the small interactive-boost recency table.

WHY: #5 is explicitly documented as a best-effort HINT already --
missing a boost is never a correctness problem, only a missed
optimization (see the function's own comments). A blocking lock on
this exact path means one thread's read insert can stall waiting for
another CPU's read insert to finish touching the same small table --
pure added latency for zero benefit, on precisely the path this whole
patch series has been trying to speed up. None of this session's
benchmarks (miniio, CPDT) are multi-threaded, so this contention was
never exercised or visible in any measured number so far -- but real
Android usage has many concurrent app threads reading at once, where
this would matter. Uncontended case (the common one, including
everything measured this session) is completely unchanged: trylock
succeeds immediately, identical behavior to the blocking version.
Contended case now returns "no boost" immediately instead of waiting.

spin_trylock_irqsave is already used elsewhere in this exact file
(the pre-existing bq_lock trylock), so this isn't introducing a new
API pattern to the codebase.

NOT submitted upstream. NOT compiled/tested against a real
android14-6.1 tree yet -- verified only for brace/paren balance and
that spin_unlock_irqrestore (unchanged) is valid regardless of how
the lock was acquired, which it is.
